## 1. Atomic nodes (4 sections, 11 October 2026)

Index page: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/atomic-nodes (11 October 2026)

### 1.1 Pixel processor (11 October 2026)

- Category: Atomic nodes, Substance graphs. (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: outputs an image in which each pixel's value comes from a Substance function graph that the user defines, evaluated for every pixel in parallel. (11 October 2026)
- Main parameters: "Color mode" (Boolean, grayscale or color output, default not stated); "Per pixel function" (Float or Float4 function graph, default not stated). Optional "Input image #" (grayscale or color), read with a Sample color or Sample grayscale node. (11 October 2026)
- Notes: pixel position comes from a Get Float2 node set to `$pos`. Each pixel is computed without knowledge of its neighbours. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus nearest: `Cop wrangle` (VEX per pixel, not a function graph). Status: documented_only. (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/pixel-processor (11 October 2026)

### 1.2 Value processor (11 October 2026)

- Category: Atomic nodes, Substance graphs. (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: evaluates a Substance function graph and outputs a single value that can be used inside a Substance graph. (11 October 2026)
- Main parameters: "Value processor function" (any available value type, default not stated). Optional "Input image #" (grayscale or color), read with Sample color or Sample grayscale. (11 October 2026)
- Notes: same idea as the Pixel processor, but one value instead of one function per pixel. The page says it can be more optimised than the Pixel processor. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus nearest: none. Copernicus works on layers and has no single-value node. Status: documented_only. (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/value-processor (11 October 2026)

### 1.3 Normal (11 October 2026)

- Category: Atomic nodes, Substance graphs. (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: computes a tangent-space normal map from a grayscale image treated as a height map. (11 October 2026)
- Main parameters: "Intensity" (Float, default not stated; values above 100 have little extra effect, depending on the input); "Normal format" (Boolean, flips the Y coordinate, OpenGL convention); "Alpha channel content" (Boolean, "Fill Alpha With Input" or "Force Alpha To 1"). (11 October 2026)
- Input: "Input" (grayscale, primary). Output: a tangent-space normal map. (11 October 2026)
- Notes: the page lists Normal Sobel and Height To Normal World Units as alternatives. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus nearest: `Cop heighttonormal`. Status: documented_only. (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/normal (11 October 2026)

### 1.4 Channels shuffle (11 October 2026)

- Category: Atomic nodes, Substance graphs. (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: rearranges the colour channels of one or two input images into an output image. (11 October 2026)
- Main parameters: "Red channel", "Green channel", "Blue channel", "Alpha channel" (all Integer, each selects the source channel; defaults not stated). (11 October 2026)
- Inputs: "Input 1" (colour or grayscale, primary); "Input 2" (colour or grayscale, secondary). (11 October 2026)
- Notes: grayscale inputs are treated as colour, so R, G, B and A return the same value. The page recommends RGBA Merge, RGBA Split, Alpha Merge or Alpha Split for simple packing. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus nearest: `Cop channelswap`. Status: documented_only. (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/channel-shuffle (11 October 2026)

